--TEST--
Core\IO::isWritable names `fs.write` and is refused at the door for a path the configuration does not cover, whether or not that path exists — the capability is a separate and earlier gate than the operating system's own answer, so a program cannot sweep a boolean across the filesystem to map its own grant — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`, spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `fs.read` covers this directory and `fs.write` covers nothing. That splits
// the pair: `isReadable` answers, `isWritable` refuses. A member that folded
// the capability into its boolean would answer `false` on both lines below and
// look entirely reasonable doing it -- while handing any program a probe for
// the configuration it is running under.
if (Core\IO::isReadable("seed.txt")) {
    echo "seed.txt: readable\n";
} else {
    echo "seed.txt: not readable\n";
}

// `Here::path` returns a literal resolved from this file's folder, and each
// name below is joined onto it, so the door is handed an absolute path.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
string $dir = Here::path('.');
array<string> $paths = ["seed.txt", "missing.txt"];
int $n = Core\Arr::count($paths) as int;
string $needed = " needs the capability `fs.write` for ";

int $refused = 0;
int $namingFsWrite = 0;

int $i = 0;
for (; $i < $n; $i = $i + 1) {
    string $path = Core\Path::join($dir, $paths[$i]);
    try {
        bool $may = Core\IO::isWritable($path);
        echo "isWritable answered for ", $path, "\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        if ($denied->message == "Core\\IO::isWritable" . $needed . $path . ", which is not granted\nhelp: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`") {
            $namingFsWrite = $namingFsWrite + 1;
        }
    }
}

// Counted over both paths, because the load-bearing half of the claim is that
// the refusal does not depend on whether the name is there: a member that
// refused only the file that exists would leak the existence of every other
// one through the difference.
echo "paths: ", $n as string,
     ", refused: ", $refused as string,
     ", naming fs.write: ", $namingFsWrite as string, "\n";
--FILE seed.txt--
kept
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--EXPECT--
seed.txt: readable
paths: 2, refused: 2, naming fs.write: 2
